<header><p class="st-kicker">duchy · Scandinavia and the Baltic</p><h1 class="st-title">Grand Duchy of Finland</h1><p class="st-meta">1809 CE – 1917 CE · capital Helsinki</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Autonomous Finland within the Russian Empire, where Finnish national identity, institutions and the first female suffrage in Europe took shape.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> conquered <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-finland">Finland</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finnish-war">Finnish War</a> of 1808-1809. At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diet-of-porvoo">Diet of Porvoo</a> in 1809 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-i-of-russia">Alexander I</a> promised to respect <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland's</a> laws, Lutheran religion and estates, and Finland became a grand duchy with the tsar as grand duke. The Treaty of Fredrikshamn confirmed the transfer, and in 1812 the capital moved from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turku">Turku</a> to Helsinki.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-finland">Finland</a> kept its Swedish-era laws and had its own Senate, civil service, army for a time and, from 1860, its own currency, the markka. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-ii-of-russia">Alexander II</a> convened the Diet regularly and allowed Finnish to become an official language. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elias-lonnrot">Elias Lönnrot's</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalevala">Kalevala</a>, published in 1835, inspired the national movement. In 1906 a unicameral parliament was created, elected by universal suffrage including women, the first in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>From 1899 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russification">Russification</a> threatened Finnish autonomy, and Governor-General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nikolai-bobrikov">Bobrikov</a> was assassinated in 1904. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-revolution">Russian Revolution</a>, the Finnish parliament declared independence on 6 December 1917, and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/russian-sfsr">Soviet Russia</a> recognised it, creating the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-finland">Republic of Finland</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/grand-duchy-of-finland.jpg" alt="Grand Duchy of Finland" width="320" height="324"><figcaption>Image: Ориенталист · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Grand_Principality_of_Finland_(1914)_and_Russian_Empire.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1809 CE – 1917 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>duchy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Helsinki</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Alexander I, Alexander II, Nicholas II</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Lutheran Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Finnish, Swedish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/finland">Finland</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Finland, Suomen suuriruhtinaskunta, Storfurstendömet Finland</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1863&amp;focus=grand-duchy-of-finland" aria-label="Grand Duchy of Finland on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5201 163 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Grand Duchy of Finland lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5521 419L5381 440M5521 419L6487 457M5521 419L5521 419"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5381 440h0M6487 457h0M5521 419h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5521 419h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5521 419h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Helsinki. <a href="/atlas?year=1863&amp;focus=grand-duchy-of-finland">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
